I can probably find the quote, but I am not sure it is really relevant.=20
  But when someone repeatedly tells me I am wrong, I am less likely to=20
keep trying.  I am sure that I am not the only one this holds for, and I=20
am pretty sure that I would misuse xorlo, if only because I don't know=20
when NOT to use "lo".

> I believe And may be thinking of something like "mi nelci le nu limna"
> for "I like swimming". The problem with "le" here is that when you say
> "I like swimming" you don't have a particular event of swimming in mind,
> so why would you use "le"?

le is not necessarily singular (or plural), but I certainly have some=20
concept of what events of swimming (that I am fond of) are, and I am,=20
referring to one or more of such events.

  "le nu limna" is supposed to be a particular
> event of swimming that the speaker has in mind.
>
> (Nowadays many people would probably rather say "mi nelci lo ka limna",
> but the change from "nu" to "ka" is not from xorlo.)

But of course I don't know that my concept of swimming and/or its=20
properties is the same as yours.  Since I still associate "lo" with=20
veridicality, and there are likely some events of swimming that I would=20
not nelci, I would have trouble saying "lo".

